# Review package: batch 7, slice delta

## 4b1122a Keep a freed name from its next owner while its old route may live, and refuse an old owner's tunnel under a new binding

Batch 7's fix round, Critical. A freed name read as free while its old route was in the 10 s pending window, a registration
landed after a release or a ban had passed its login check, and a new connection was judged by the name alone. So company B
could be handed acme.hornhoof.org while its visitors reached company A's machine.

- name_live now counts a route the plugin let through and frps does not show yet, by the same window as R2.1, so that
  GET /names says taken and POST /bindings answers 409 (R1.5).
- A route's row lands only while the binding that authorized it stands, in one statement: a release, a key change or a ban
  since the login check refuses the NewProxy and moves no generation (R1.1).
- NewUserConn also asks whether the proxy's owner logged in with this binding's key, current or retired in this lifetime, so
  that an old owner's tunnel is refused under a new binding at once (R7.4). A tunnel of a changed key stays (R6.1).

Minors. `ban --cut acme` no longer blocks the word --cut and leaves acme live: the option goes first and the name must be
able to be one. RelayServer closes a connection's SQLite connection when its thread ends. The help line of ban says what a ban
does; the README says frps also asks about the edge's connections and that the relay reads what a tunnel logged in with.

Lock order: unchanged. create_lock and route_lock(name) never nest, each may wait on the writer queue last, and whoever holds
the writer takes neither.

Ruling: name_live takes no route_lock and create_binding holds none across its check and store, against the review's suggested fix - why: the statement's own guard closes the race, also against relay.py admin, another process no lock reaches; a public GET then writes nothing and cannot grow _route_locks by any name asked; after check_name finds no binding no registration can land - cost if wrong: a name goes to a new owner while a registration is in flight, which NewUserConn still refuses for https and stcp.
Ruling: NewUserConn reads the owner's key where Plan 1 Task 10 lets the name alone decide - why: a tunnel can still find its name bound again: admin add does not ask frps, and frps may show a route after the window; the read stays one SELECT by the primary key, plus one on ping_keys only for a tunnel of a changed key - cost if wrong: a real tunnel with no key in its login metas is refused as freed, and every real-frps test here would show it.
Ruling: a pending row holds a freed name for the rest of its 10 s even when frps shows the route gone - why: this is R2.1's own rule, since frps keeps an old offline record until the new one shows - cost if wrong: an owner who frees and rebinds within 10 s of a registration sees 409 taken for those seconds.
Ruling: left open, http-mode routes - why: frps calls NewUserConn only for proxies with their own listeners, so an http route under a new binding is stopped only by R1.5, which admin add skips (hornhoof-relay runs it without the dashboard's password) and which a route shown after the window escapes - cost if wrong: an operator's add over a live http tunnel, or an frps stalled over 10 s, sends the new owner's visitors to the old owner until that tunnel goes.

